    "https://careers.emich.edu/jobs/dir-staff-command-programs-ypsilanti-michigan-united-states": {
      "title": "Dir, Staff & Command Programs",
      "description": "Dir, Staff & Command Programs - Ypsilanti, Michigan, United States Home Working at EMU Benefits & Wellness About HR Existing Applicant Log-in Kiosk mode Dir, Staff & Command Programs Ypsilanti, Michigan, United States Placeholder1 College of Arts & Sciences Staff Full Time Opening on: Sep 24 2026 Closing on: Oct 9 2026 Add to favorites View favorites Title: Dir, Staff & Command Programs Employee Classification: AP - Administrative Professional Pay Grade: AP 05 Division: College of Arts and Sciences Subdivision: School of Staff and Command Campus Location: Main Campus General Summary The Director, Staff and Command Programs directs specific programs for Staff and Command across multiple locations within in the College of Arts and Sciences. The director manages all administrative and operational activities in support of this operation, ensures student success, manages public safety community partnerships, and explores its possible national and global expansion. Principal Duties and Responsibilities Direct and supervise the preparation of budgets, proposals, status reports, and financial statements related to Staff and Command activities. Plan and tightly manage a departmental budget and resources efficiently, ensuring consistency with university policy. Expand Staff and Command at EMU, including identifying and developing revenue models and/or grant resources for Staff and Command projects and initiatives. Work with faculty members and administrators on federal, state and other grants for funding Staff and Command activities. Develop curriculum for new learning opportunities for students in the proposed programs. Construct grant and gift applications relevant to assigned projects, and collaborate with the Foundation to format and execute fundraising campaigns. Initiate and manage new community/university-based programs based on institutional directives, regional data, and market analysis. Develop the Staff and Command strategic plan, and support CAS in other business/community-based initiatives or research. Oversee daily and long-term operations of Staff and Command outreach which includes development of market analysis, vendor relations including contract management, and engagement and outreach with EMU academic departments and programs. Direct and manage operations of Staff and Command which include the coordination among all offices and systems involved in the program, and the management of advisory board membership and meetings with local community organizations. Build and maintain positive relationships with academic and administrative units within the University. Ensure a positive, diverse, and inclusive learning environment. Seek out and collaborate with staff, faculty and alumni in Staff and Command. Act as a campus-wide liaison for relations outside the university, and serve as liaison for various organizations, local, regional committees, and districts in SE Michigan and beyond. Conduct administrative duties as needed including managing other personnel and supervising their professional activities. Supervise professional staff and student assistants, and manage a balanced department budget consistent with university policy. Recruit, hire, train and supervise staff to deliver classes for the Staff and Command programs. Ensure the success of graduate and undergraduate students enrolled in the Fire and Police Staff and Command Programs through professional mentoring and advising. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Knowledge of research methodology and data gathering for determining policy and program change, as well as related policy or program implementation, typically acquired through the completion of a related master's degree is required. A minimum of three years of documented work experience in one or more of the following areas: strategic planning, public policy, community development, or Police/Fire leadership, is required. Documented proficiency with external partnerships/collaborations (e.g. business, public districts, government and non-profit) is required. A minimum of five years of experience in project management, conference/event planning, curriculum, writing, contract development, and administrative and office management experience, preferably within an academic unit or environment, is required. Documented experience building a pipeline for college enrollment and assisting students in advocating for initiatives important to the university. In-depth knowledge of the operation and culture of the police and fire-fighting communities is required. Outstanding oral and written communication, leadership, and collaboration skills are required. Demonstrated evidence of problem-solving, conflict management, and interpersonal skills is required. Demonstrated evidence of implementing quality improvements and providing excellent customer service in a fast-paced environment is required. Knowledge of accounting and financial principles, and business administration is required. Preferred Qualifications: A master's degree in Educational Leadership, Public Administration, Public Policy, Business, Non-profit Administration, or a related field is preferred. Previous experience working with grant funded programs, including grant writing experience, is strongly preferred. Experience working in higher education is preferred. Special Instructions: Appointment Percentage: 100% Apply Now! Want to hear about jobs like this one? Job Alert acf385c4 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments Placeholder1 Placeholder1 Categories College of Arts & Sciences College of Arts & Sciences First Name (required) 1165783c Last Name (required) 391dd04c Email (required) 206fc620 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs Institutional Research An